Overlay patch 0030 - CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone, so ProMotion can reach 120.

CLASS: (a) program-baseline parity.

WHY
---
iOS caps an app at 60 Hz on a ProMotion display unless its Info.plist opts in.
Without the opt-in, Settings > iOS > Max Frame Rate 120 (overlay 0021/0023) can
never be reached on the hardware that has the panel for it: the engine would
happily interpolate for 120 and the compositor would present 60.

REV 2 (round 16) - THE KEY NAME WAS WRONG, AND IT IS THE WHOLE 60 Hz STORY
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Rev 1 shipped `CADisableMinimumFrameDuration`. That is the IPAD key (iOS 10.3,
iPad Pro). **On an iPhone the key is `CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone`**
(iOS 15, iPhone 13 Pro onwards), and nothing else opts a phone out of the 60 Hz
minimum frame duration. The user's 0.0.0.9 device read is exactly what the wrong
key looks like: interpolation target 120 (`ipf=4.00`), presents pinned to 60
(`wall_ms p50=16.69` - the 60 Hz period to two decimal places) and therefore the
game ticking at 15 Hz instead of 30 with the audio thread starving behind it
(961 underruns).

Two independent confirmations, both already on this machine:

  * SDL 2 says so in its own source, in the comment above the high-refresh
    display link it only creates when the desktop mode is above 60:
    `build-sim/_deps/sdl2-src/src/video/uikit/SDL_uikitviewcontroller.m:122-125`
    - "To enable this on phones, you should add the following line to Info.plist:
    `<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key> <true/>`".
  * **Every sibling ships the OnPhone key**, and the SoH port measured a locked
    120 with it: `Shipwright-ios/scripts/gen-patch-0004-0005.py`,
    `Lighthouse-ios/scripts/gen-patch-0004-0005.py`, and
    the SoH port's measurement log ("wall p50 8.32 ms, rendered fps
    119.9-120.0 ... the CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone plist key + SDL
    display path + engine pacer compose correctly out of the box").

Rev 2 therefore ships BOTH keys - the OnPhone one because that is what an iPhone
reads, and the original because it is what an iPad Pro reads and this bundle
installs on both. Neither has any effect on a 60 Hz device.

This has been a follow-up line in `docs/parity-checklist.md` since round 11,
deliberately not bundled blind. It lands now because round 15 also makes the
interpolation target follow the display's refresh rate (overlay 0023 rev 2):
with that in place, this key is the difference between a fresh install running
at 60 on an iPhone Air and running at 120.

It is one boolean and it cannot be verified on the simulator, which has no
ProMotion panel - `xcrun simctl` reports a 60 Hz display. What IS verified on
the simulator is that the key is present in the built bundle's Info.plist and
that the app still launches, which is what `scripts/build-sim.sh` plus a
`plutil -extract` assertion cover. The 120 itself is a device reading.

WHERE THE HUNK SITS
-------------------
`ios/plist.in` is edited by three other patches: overlay 0005 at pristine lines
5-11 and 18-30 (branding and version), overlay 0001 at 36-41 (the UIScene
manifest), overlay 0009 at 54-58 (the URL scheme). This hunk is anchored on
`UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance` (pristine 45-46) and so spans 44-49 -
clear of all three, which is what keeps apply-overlay.sh's reverse-probe
("already applied") working when two patches share a context line.

Match-count asserted against the pristine vendor state.

--- a/ios/plist.in
+++ b/ios/plist.in
@@ -44,6 +44,20 @@
 	<true/>
 	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
 	<false/>
+	<!-- PAPERBOAT_IOS (overlay 0030 rev 2): opt out of the 60 Hz minimum frame
+	     duration. Without this iOS refuses to present faster than 60 on a
+	     ProMotion panel no matter what the app asks for, so Settings > iOS >
+	     Max Frame Rate 120 could never be reached on the hardware that has the
+	     display for it.
+	     BOTH keys, deliberately: an iPHONE reads ...OnPhone (iOS 15, iPhone 13
+	     Pro onwards) and reading the iPad key alone is what pinned 0.0.0.9 to 60
+	     with the engine interpolating for 120 (ipf 4, tps 15 - half speed). An
+	     iPad Pro reads the shorter key. Neither does anything on a 60 Hz
+	     device. -->
+	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDurationOnPhone</key>
+	<true/>
+	<key>CADisableMinimumFrameDuration</key>
+	<true/>
 	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
 	<array>
 		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
